WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.776 --> 00:00:15.300
We imprisoned in the tunnel of ice.

2
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.800
Ned banged the table with his fist.

3
00:00:18.581 --> 00:00:21.021
Conseil stayed quiet while I watched the captain.

4
00:00:21.861 --> 00:00:24.462
Once again Nemo's face betrayed no emotion.

5
00:00:24.482 --> 00:00:29.864
"Gentlemen," said Captain Nemo, "there are two ways of dying in this situation."

6
00:00:30.856 --> 00:00:35.217
He made these remarks as calmly as a mathematics professor lecturing his pupils would.

7
00:00:35.237 --> 00:00:40.699
"The first is to be crushed by the ice, and the second is to run out of air."

8
00:00:40.719 --> 00:00:44.060
"We don't have to worry about running out of air, do we?"

9
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:44.880
I asked.

10
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.881
"Our reservoirs are full."

11
00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:52.442
"We have spent the last day underwater and already the submarine's air needs to be renewed.

12
00:00:53.642 --> 00:00:59.652
Obviously we can't get to the surface to refresh the air, and in two days—maybe three—we will have

13
00:00:59.672 --> 00:01:01.433
exhausted the supply in our reservoirs."

14
00:01:01.453 --> 00:01:05.814
"Well, Captain, can we get out of this situation by then?"

15
00:01:06.394 --> 00:01:06.754
I asked.

16
00:01:06.774 --> 00:01:10.015
"We will certainly try," he replied.

17
00:01:10.035 --> 00:01:13.456
"I'm going to run the Nautilus aground on the iceberg below us.

18
00:01:14.256 --> 00:01:18.017
My men will then put on their diving suits and attack the ice wherever it's the thinnest."

19
00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:22.898
Captain Nemo left the gallery, and the submarine soon began to sink slowly.

20
00:01:22.918 --> 00:01:29.684
"My friends," I said to Conseil and Ned, "we're in serious trouble here, but I rely on your

21
00:01:29.744 --> 00:01:30.885
courage and good spirits."

22
00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:37.546
"Professor," replied Ned, "I'm as handy with a pickax as I am with a harpoon, so if I

23
00:01:37.586 --> 00:01:39.667
can be useful to the captain, I'm willing to help him."

24
00:01:39.687 --> 00:01:42.408
"He won't refuse your aid," I said.

25
00:01:42.428 --> 00:01:43.728
"Come with me, Ned."

26
00:01:44.948 --> 00:01:48.909
I led him to the room where Captain Nemo and some crewmen were putting on their diving suits.

27
00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.590
I told the captain of Ned's offer, which the captain accepted.

28
00:01:53.991 --> 00:01:58.112
When Ned was dressed, I returned to the gallery and found Conseil looking out the windows.

29
00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.301
A few minutes later we saw a dozen men set foot on the ice.

30
00:02:03.321 --> 00:02:08.023
Captain Nemo drilled holes in the ice to determine where it was the thinnest, and then the men

31
00:02:08.063 --> 00:02:09.723
attacked the ice with their pickaxes.

32
00:02:10.784 --> 00:02:13.885
After two hours of hard work, Ned came in, exhausted.

33
00:02:14.845 --> 00:02:18.626
He and his comrades were replaced by new workers, including Conseil and me.

34
00:02:19.586 --> 00:02:24.808
The submarine's crew worked for twelve hours and managed to clear away only a small patch of ice.

35
00:02:25.916 --> 00:02:29.057
At this rate it would take us at least four days to get out of the tunnel.

36
00:02:29.077 --> 00:02:35.320
"And even if we do get out of this infernal prison," said Ned, "don't forget that we're still

37
00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.360
under the iceberg.

38
00:02:37.101 --> 00:02:40.962
Who knows how long it will be until we reach open water and can rise to the surface!"

39
00:02:42.023 --> 00:02:45.784
When I went out to work the next morning, it seemed as if the walls of the tunnel

40
00:02:45.824 --> 00:02:47.225
were starting to close in on us.

41
00:02:48.745 --> 00:02:52.767
I didn't say anything to Ned or Conseil, but I did mention it later to the captain.

42
00:02:52.787 --> 00:02:56.565
"I'm aware of that," he replied, as calm as ever.

43
00:02:56.585 --> 00:03:02.367
"It's one more danger, but the only thing to do is remove the ice faster than it is

44
00:03:02.407 --> 00:03:03.768
building up on the tunnel walls."

45
00:03:04.848 --> 00:03:07.949
I used my pickax even more vigorously the next time I went out.

46
00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:13.212
To tell the truth, it was a relief to work because the air in my diving suit was

47
00:03:13.252 --> 00:03:15.553
now so much better than the air onboard the Nautilus.

48
00:03:16.633 --> 00:03:20.795
But as soon as I stepped onto the ice the next day, I noticed that the side walls

49
00:03:20.835 --> 00:03:23.456
and the surface below us had visibly thickened with ice.

50
00:03:24.524 --> 00:03:27.306
The submarine would be crushed before we could free it!

51
00:03:28.346 --> 00:03:31.488
I was filled with despair and almost dropped my pickax.

52
00:03:32.328 --> 00:03:35.430
I found Captain Nemo and signaled to him that we needed to talk.

53
00:03:36.891 --> 00:03:40.133
We reentered the Nautilus, where we removed our diving suits.

54
00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:47.637
"We must take desperate steps, Professor Aronnax," said Captain Nemo, "or we will be sealed up in this

55
00:03:47.837 --> 00:03:49.117
ice as if in cement.

56
00:03:49.518 --> 00:03:53.540
If only my Nautilus were strong enough to bear this pressure without being crushed."

57
00:03:54.528 --> 00:03:56.690
"How much longer will the air last in the reservoirs?"

58
00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:57.751
I wanted to know.

59
00:03:58.752 --> 00:03:59.853
The captain looked me in the eye.

60
00:03:59.873 --> 00:04:01.974
"They will be empty tomorrow."

61
00:04:03.035 --> 00:04:05.837
Overcome with terror, I broke out into a cold sweat.

62
00:04:06.518 --> 00:04:08.900
Captain Nemo, meanwhile, appeared to be thinking hard.

63
00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.702
"Boiling water!"

64
00:04:11.122 --> 00:04:11.522
he muttered.

65
00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:12.903
"Boiling water?"

66
00:04:13.224 --> 00:04:13.624
I asked.

67
00:04:13.644 --> 00:04:15.866
"Yes, Professor Aronnax.

68
00:04:16.206 --> 00:04:18.028
We're in a relatively confined space.

69
00:04:18.388 --> 00:04:23.132
Wouldn't pumping boiling water into the sea raise its temperature enough to stop this ice from forming?"

70
00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.521
"I suppose so," I said.

71
00:04:25.541 --> 00:04:26.882
"Let's try it."

72
00:04:28.282 --> 00:04:32.104
Seawater was constantly being taken onboard and purified for drinking water.

73
00:04:33.065 --> 00:04:37.407
The captain now ordered the crew to heat this water to boiling and pump it back out into

74
00:04:37.427 --> 00:04:37.808
the sea.

75
00:04:38.908 --> 00:04:42.630
Slowly the temperature of the water surrounding the submarine began to rise.

76
00:04:43.711 --> 00:04:46.352
Five hours later the temperature had risen by two degrees.

77
00:04:47.393 --> 00:04:52.936
It continued to rise overnight until it was close to the freezing point—or melting point in this case.

78
00:04:53.952 --> 00:04:57.333
"We won't be crushed by the ice now," said Captain Nemo the next morning.

79
00:04:57.353 --> 00:05:01.695
"And we've made some real progress in removing the ice around the submarine."

80
00:05:02.676 --> 00:05:04.396
But now the reservoirs were out of air.

81
00:05:05.237 --> 00:05:08.978
As the day wore on, I felt as if a terrible weight were pressing on me.

82
00:05:09.979 --> 00:05:12.920
It was a relief when it was time to go outside and attack the ice.

83
00:05:14.341 --> 00:05:17.762
Back inside the submarine, I felt myself losing consciousness.

84
00:05:18.462 --> 00:05:19.523
Conseil took my hand.

85
00:05:19.543 --> 00:05:24.961
"If only I didn't have to breathe, I could leave more air for you," he told me.

86
00:05:26.061 --> 00:05:28.042
His unselfishness brought tears to my eyes.

87
00:05:28.062 --> 00:05:31.704
"Listen, Master," he said, cocking his head.

88
00:05:31.724 --> 00:05:34.565
"It sounds as if the water tanks are filling."

89
00:05:35.525 --> 00:05:36.866
He got up and looked out the windows.

90
00:05:36.886 --> 00:05:39.087
"And all the men have come inside."

91
00:05:40.127 --> 00:05:43.569
All of a sudden there was a cracking sound, and I knew what had happened.

92
00:05:43.589 --> 00:05:47.510
"We've broken through the ice below us," I whispered.

93
00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:48.991
"We're free!"

94
00:05:49.691 --> 00:05:50.932
Conseil shouted hoarsely.

95
00:05:52.032 --> 00:05:56.833
We had escaped from the tunnel, but how long would it be before we reached open water and

96
00:05:56.873 --> 00:05:58.054
could rise to the surface?

97
00:05:59.154 --> 00:06:00.894
After a while I no longer cared.

98
00:06:01.655 --> 00:06:04.955
Stretched out on a couch, I felt as if I were going to die.

99
00:06:06.356 --> 00:06:11.877
I could not see or hear or move a muscle, and I have no idea how many hours

100
00:06:11.937 --> 00:06:12.678
I spent like that.

101
00:06:13.678 --> 00:06:15.178
Suddenly I revived!

102
00:06:15.938 --> 00:06:18.079
Some breaths of air were filling my lungs.

103
00:06:18.859 --> 00:06:20.480
Had we risen to the surface of the ocean?

104
00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:26.288
No, Conseil and Ned, my two brave friends, were sacrificing themselves to save me.

105
00:06:27.329 --> 00:06:32.033
A little bit of air remained in the bottom of one breathing apparatus from a diving suit, and

106
00:06:32.053 --> 00:06:32.934
they were giving it to me.

107
00:06:33.975 --> 00:06:37.898
I wanted to push the air tank away, but they held it to my face, and for a

108
00:06:37.978 --> 00:06:40.000
few moments, I breathed freely.

109
00:06:41.021 --> 00:06:43.423
I looked at the clock and saw that it was eleven o'clock.

110
00:06:44.544 --> 00:06:48.768
Regaining some of my strength, I forced myself to get up and looked at the instruments.

111
00:06:49.792 --> 00:06:55.797
The Nautilus was twisting and turning through the water, moving at a rapid pace, and soon we were

112
00:06:55.837 --> 00:06:57.698
only twenty feet below the surface.

113
00:06:58.759 --> 00:06:59.980
But where was Captain Nemo?

114
00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:01.661
I wondered desperately.

115
00:07:02.441 --> 00:07:04.803
Were he and his crew even still alive?
